. /*
> (i.) Interpret the coefficient on shall in regression (2). Is this estimate
> large or small in a real-world sense?
> 
> Answer:
> - The coefficient on `shall` in regression (2) is approximately -0.368.
> - Interpretation: Holding incarceration rate, density, average income, total
>   population, and demographic shares constant, states with a shall-issue
>   concealed weapons law have a violent crime rate that is approximately 36.8%
>   lower (or more precisely, 100 * (exp(-0.3684) - 1) = -30.8% lower) than sta
> tes
>   without such a law.
> - Real-world magnitude: A ~31-37% reduction in violent crime is a very large
>   effect in a real-world sense.
> 
> (ii.) Does adding the control variables in regression (2) change the estimate
> d
> effect of a shall-issue law in regression (1) as measured by statistical
> significance? As measured by the real-world significance of the estimated
> coefficient?
> 
> Answer:
> - Statistical significance: In both regression (1) (coef = -0.443, SE = 0.048
> ,
>   t = -9.15) and regression (2) (coef = -0.368, SE = 0.035, t = -10.59), the
>   coefficient on `shall` is highly statistically significant at the 1% level.
> - Real-world significance: Adding controls attenuates the point estimate slig
> htly
>   from -0.443 (-44.3%) to -0.368 (-36.8%), but both estimates remain very lar
> ge
>   in magnitude and economically meaningful.
> 
> (iii.) Suggest a variable that varies across states but plausibly varies
> little—or not at all—over time and that could cause omitted variable bias in
> regression (2).
> 
> Answer:
> - Examples include underlying cultural attitudes toward guns and violence,
>   long-standing quality/organization of state police and judicial institution
> s,
>   geography/urban structure (e.g., rural vs. metropolitan concentration not
>   captured by state-wide density), or historical gang presence. These factors
>   differ across states, are largely time-invariant, affect violent crime rate
> s,
>   and are correlated with whether a state enacts a shall-issue law.
> */
. 
. /*===========================================================================
