> Answer:
> - Adding time fixed effects reduces the magnitude of the `shall` coefficient
>   even further, from -0.046 to -0.028 (clustered SE = 0.041, t = -0.69,
>   p = 0.496), which remains statistically insignificant.
> - The time fixed effects are jointly highly significant (F-test p-value < 0.0
> 01).
> - The regression with both state and time fixed effects is more credible beca
> use
>   national crime rates fluctuated substantially between 1977 and 1999 (e.g., 
> the
>   national crime decline in the 1990s due to macroeconomic trends, crack-coca
> ine
>   epidemic dynamics, federal policies, and national policing changes) at the
>   same time that more states were adopting shall-issue laws.
> */
