. /*
> Summary of (d):
> - For both `ln(rob)` and `ln(mur)`, the pattern mirrors `ln(vio)`:
>   * In Pooled OLS (Models 1 & 2), `shall` has a large, negative, and highly
>     statistically significant coefficient:
>     - Robbery: -0.773 (no controls) and -0.529 (with controls)
>     - Murder:  -0.473 (no controls) and -0.313 (with controls)
>   * Once state fixed effects (Model 3) and state + time fixed effects (Model 
> 4)
>     are included, the estimated coefficients shrink near zero and become
>     statistically insignificant:
>     - Robbery: -0.008 (State FE) and +0.027 (State + Year FE)
>     - Murder:  -0.061 (State FE) and -0.015 (State + Year FE)
> */
. 
. /*===========================================================================
> ==
> (e) In your view, what are the most important remaining threats to the intern
> al
> validity of this regression analysis?
> 
> Answer:
> 1. Simultaneous Causality / Endogenous Policy Adoption: States may pass (or
>    repeal) shall-issue laws in response to recent state-specific surges or
>    trends in violent crime.
> 2. Time-Varying Omitted Variables: Unobserved state-level factors that change
>    over time—such as state-specific policing reforms, changes in local drug
>    markets, sentencing laws, or state-specific economic shocks—are not absorb
> ed
>    by state fixed effects (which only capture time-invariant state factors) o
> r
>    year fixed effects (which only capture national shocks).
> 3. Lagged Incarceration Rate Endogeneity: `incarc_rate` is lagged one year an
> d
>    depends on past crime rates, which can introduce bias in a fixed-effects
>    specification.
> 
> (f) Based on your analysis, what conclusions would you draw about the effects
> of concealed weapons laws on these crime rates?
> 
> Answer:
> - The large negative relationship between shall-issue laws and violent crime
>   found in simple cross-sectional / pooled OLS regressions is driven by omitt
> ed
>   variable bias from unobserved time-invariant state differences.
> - Once we control for state fixed effects and national time trends, there is 
> no
>   statistically significant evidence that shall-issue concealed weapons laws
>   reduce violent crime, robbery, or murder rates.
> =============================================================================
> */
